INT. GLASSBLOWER'S STUDIO, MURANO - LATE AFTERNOON

The furnace roars. Orange light on every wall. CHIARA BELLINI-OKADA (30s, leather apron, burn scars on both forearms) turns a rod of molten glass. ELIAS WARDLAW (30s, rumpled linen, a stranger to heat) stands too close to the bench with a notebook.

ELIAS
(to the room)
Is it always this hot?

CHIARA
Always. You'll melt before the glass does.

They laugh. The glass swells on the pipe, a glowing teardrop, and for a moment the studio feels very small and very kind.

He steps in to see. She swings the rod without looking. The blob drips to the floor. They both leap back, collide, and fall in a heap, his notebook skidding under the bench.

ELIAS
I am so sorry.

CHIARA
You should be.

She is laughing again. He is not.

ELIAS
(muttering)
Stupid. Stupid. Stupid.

She reaches over, tilts his chin toward the light, and inspects a small red mark on his jaw.

CHIARA
You're burned.

ELIAS
I'm fine.

CHIARA
Hold still.

They freeze. Her thumb rests on his cheek. The furnace roars. Neither breathes.

CHIARA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
My father says never look at the fire. Only at the glass.

ELIAS
And where do I look?

She doesn't answer. He does not look at the glass.
